# Accepted-risk CVEs — every entry needs a justification, an advisory link, and a revisit
# condition. This file is what lets the SCA stage run as a HARD GATE without going permanently
# red on findings that have no fix we can take. Passed to Trivy explicitly via --ignorefile
# (Jenkinsfile SCA stage and the local rescan command in STARTUP.md).

# hibernate-core 5.4.33.Final — second-order SQL injection in InlineIdsOrClauseBuilder.
# https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-0603
# No fixed 5.x release exists; the fix line is Hibernate 6.x, which requires Java 17 — this
# project is pinned to Java 8 (maven.compiler 1.8). The vulnerable class is a multi-table
# bulk-id helper used only for bulk UPDATE/DELETE over inheritance hierarchies; this app has
# no entity inheritance and issues no bulk HQL mutations, so the code path is unreachable.
# Revisit: if the project ever moves off the Java 8 pin, bump to Hibernate 6.x and delete this.
CVE-2026-0603

# spring-security-crypto 5.8.16 — BCryptPasswordEncoder.matches() returns true for any
# >72-byte password whose first 72 bytes match (BCrypt silently truncates its input).
# https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2025-22228
# Trivy's listed fix for the 5.x line (5.8.18) is a commercial-only Tanzu release; 5.8.16 is
# the latest OSS 5.8.x on Maven Central, and the 6.x fix line requires Java 17 (this project
# is pinned to Java 8). Mitigated in code instead: UserService rejects passwords longer than
# 72 UTF-8 bytes BEFORE they reach BCrypt (authenticate and updateProfile), so the truncation
# path is unreachable. Revisit: off the Java 8 pin, bump to Spring Security 6.4.4+ and delete.
CVE-2025-22228
